# NOTE (measured 2026-08-31): this was originally gated on a *persist.*
# property set from vendor.prop. That can never fire on this device: 7 of the 8
# persist.* properties declared in the built /vendor/build.prop are unset at
# runtime (checked with getprop), because persist.* values are sourced from
# persistent storage, not from build.prop. ro.vendor.* does load, and shell
# cannot set a vendor property anyway (property.te neverallow blocks
# coredomain), so nothing is lost by making it read-only.
# Arm the touch controller's double-tap wake gesture.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE NODE CONTRACT (read from the driver, not guessed)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# touchscreen_mmi_class.c gesture_store() parses the buffer with
#   sscanf(buf, "%d", &value)
# -- format "%d", which in the kernel's vsscanf() is fixed base 10 (base is only
# auto-detected for "%i"). So the value written must be the DECIMAL spelling of
# the command byte. Writing "0x31" parses as 0 and falls into the default case
# ("unsupport gesture mode type"). The accepted commands are:
#
#   16 (0x10)  zero  tap disable    gesture_mode_type &= ~0x01
#   17 (0x11)  zero  tap enable     gesture_mode_type |=  0x01
#   32 (0x20)  single tap disable   gesture_mode_type &= ~0x02
#   33 (0x21)  single tap enable    gesture_mode_type |=  0x02
#   48 (0x30)  DOUBLE tap disable   gesture_mode_type &= ~0x04
#   49 (0x31)  DOUBLE tap enable    gesture_mode_type |=  0x04
#
# READING the node does NOT return that state. gesture_show() prints
# pdata.supported_gesture_type as "%02x" -- the static CAPABILITY bitmask taken
# from the devicetree. arcfox declares mmi,supported_gesture_type = <0x06> on
# goodix-berlin@0, gdx-cli@0 and focaltech@1, which decodes as
#   0x02 TS_MMI_GESTURE_SINGLE | 0x04 TS_MMI_GESTURE_DOUBLE
# (0x01 zero-tap and 0x08 palm are NOT supported here). So `cat gesture` always
# prints "06" no matter what has been written. Do not use it to check state.
#
# Verified against stock: /vendor/bin/hw/com.motorola.hardware.display.touch-service
# does exactly this. Disassembly of its setGesture() shows
#   WriteStringToFile(std::to_string(value), "/sys/class/touchscreen/primary/gesture")
# i.e. the decimal spelling, and its isGestureSupported() reads the node with
# strtoll(base 0) and masks the result against the requested bit.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THE WRITE ACTUALLY DOES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing immediately -- it only sets touch_cdev->gesture_mode_type. The effect
# lands at the NEXT screen-off: ts_mmi_panel_off() (touchscreen_mmi_notif.c:56)
# takes the TS_MMI_PM_GESTURE branch instead of TS_MMI_PM_DEEPSLEEP when
# gesture_mode_type != 0, which makes the vendor driver
#   - build the IC gesture command with the double-tap bit
#     (goodix_berlin_mmi bit 7 set; goodix_gt96x_mmi bit 7 cleared from 0xFFFF),
#   - keep the touch IRQ enabled and call enable_irq_wake() on it.
# On the next double tap the driver reports BTN_TRIGGER_HAPPY6 (709) on the
# "double-tap" / "s-double-tap" input devices; the keylayouts of those names
# turn 709 into KEYCODE_WAKEUP, which is what wakes the display.
#
# The precondition (pdata.gestures_enabled / cli_gestures_enabled) is already
# satisfied -- the DT carries mmi,enable-gestures on the main panel and
# mmi,cli-enable-gestures on both cover panels, and both input devices are
# present on the running device.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY init AND NOT A HAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no client. Stock drives this node from Motorola's touch HAL, which is
# in turn driven by closed Moto system apps this ROM does not ship, so merely
# giving that HAL an SELinux domain would start a server nobody calls. The one
# framework client that exists on LineageOS is PowerManagerService's
# IPower.setMode(DOUBLE_TAP_TO_WAKE), and the power HAL on this device is a
# Motorola PREBUILT that ignores that mode -- see the notes in common.mk.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THE TRIGGER IS COMPOUND
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The write must not run before the touch drivers have probed, or it silently
# targets a path that does not exist yet and the gesture is never armed.
# sys.boot_completed is comfortably after probe (touch works on the lock screen).
# ro.vendor.arcfox.dt2w defaults to 1 from vendor.prop and is loaded from
# the persistent store during init, i.e. possibly LONG before boot_completed --
# hence a compound trigger rather than two separate ones. init re-evaluates the
# action whenever EITHER property changes, so if something later sets the
# property the matching branch fires immediately.
#
# NOTE: that "something" CANNOT be `adb shell setprop`. A coredomain is
# neverallowed from setting any vendor property (system/sepolicy/private/
# property.te:488) and shell is a coredomain, so the disable branch is reachable
# only by rebuilding vendor.prop, editing /data/property/persistent_properties,
# or a root shell. See sepolicy/vendor/property.te for the full reasoning and
# for why the property is nevertheless shell-READABLE.
#
# No new SELinux rule is needed for the write itself: these vendor rc commands
# run in vendor_init, and AOSP already grants
#   allow vendor_init { sysfs_type -sysfs_usermodehelper }:file rw_file_perms;
# (system/sepolicy/private/vendor_init.te:235). The gesture attribute is plain
# u:object_r:sysfs:s0 -- measured on this device, and it is plain sysfs on stock
# too: stock's genfscon entries for sysfs_touchclass cover /class/touchscreen and
# /devices/virtual/touchscreen, and NEITHER matches the attribute files, which
# SELinux labels by their real kernfs path under
# /devices/platform/soc/.../spi0.0/touchscreen/primary/. No genfscon is added
# here on purpose.
#
# Both panels are armed. "primary" is the inner display (goodix-berlin, spi0.0),
# "secondary" is the cover display (goodix,marseille via goodix_gt96x_mmi, or
# focaltech on other panel suppliers, spi1.0). On a flip the cover panel is the
# one the user actually taps with the phone shut.

on property:sys.boot_completed=1 && property:ro.vendor.arcfox.dt2w=1
    write /sys/class/touchscreen/primary/gesture 49
    write /sys/class/touchscreen/secondary/gesture 49

on property:sys.boot_completed=1 && property:ro.vendor.arcfox.dt2w=0
    write /sys/class/touchscreen/primary/gesture 48
    write /sys/class/touchscreen/secondary/gesture 48
